            <ArticleTitle>[CABG after patch angioplasty of the left main coronary: artery report of a case].</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>845-8</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>A 57-year-old man was admitted to our hospital because of restenosis of the left main coronary trunk (LMT) after patch angioplasty for the LMT lesion. PTCA was repeated four times during three years after patch angioplasty, but recent coronary angiogram still demonstrated 75% restenosis of the LMT lesion. Double-CABG was performed to LAD and LCX using the left internal thoracic artery and saphenous vein graft. Postoperative coronary angiogram revealed an excellent result. A careful consideration must be given to the indication of the patch angioplasty of the LMT lesion.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
